{
  "id": "course_206_le_canal_de_suez_pourquoi_il_est_strateg",
  "title": "Why is the Suez Canal so strategic?",
  "subtitle": "1869 to today",
  "subject": "comprendreLeMonde",
  "subcategory": "Conflicts & geopolitics",
  "description": "A 193-kilometer water corridor carries 12% of world trade. One ship ran aground in 2021 and stalled the planet: welcome to **Suez**.",
  "hero": {
    "image": "le_canal_de_suez",
    "ratio": "16:9",
    "hook": "193 kilometers of canal, 12% of world trade: this is the story of the most strategic passage on earth."
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "course_206_le_canal_de_suez_pourquoi_il_est_strateg_intro",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "free": true,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "On the morning of **March 23, 2021**, a sandstorm blows across northern Egypt. A giant container ship loses its heading. The photo below is taken four days later, from the International Space Station."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "ever_given_suez_canal_iss_2021",
          "ratio": "1280:886",
          "caption": "Fields on the left, desert on the right. The blue strip in the middle is the Suez Canal.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Fields on the left, desert on the right. The blue strip in the middle is the Suez Canal."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.48,
              "zoom": 2.6,
              "text": "**The ship.** It is called the *Ever Given*. It is 400 m long, as long as the Empire State Building is tall. The canal here is about 300 m wide."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.53,
              "y": 0.4,
              "zoom": 3.4,
              "text": "**The bow.** It has dug itself into the eastern bank. The stern is aground on the other side. Nothing can get past.",
              "outline": [
                {
                  "ellipse": [
                    0.572,
                    0.345,
                    0.035,
                    0.045
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "For six days, one of the busiest waterways in the world is shut by a single ship."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "suez_ship_jam_radar_2021",
          "ratio": "1280:761",
          "caption": "Two radar images of the canal's southern entrance, four days apart. Every bright dot on the water is a ship.",
          "stops": [
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.5,
              "zoom": 1,
              "text": "Two radar images of the canal's southern entrance, four days apart. Every bright dot on the water is a ship."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.25,
              "y": 0.62,
              "zoom": 2,
              "text": "**March 21.** A normal day. A few dozen ships wait their turn."
            },
            {
              "x": 0.76,
              "y": 0.62,
              "zoom": 2,
              "text": "**March 25.** The dots multiply. In the end about 400 ships are stuck, carrying oil, cars, furniture, even live animals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Each day of blockage holds up goods worth more than 9 billion dollars. How did the world come to depend on a strip of water this narrow?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
