    {
      "id": "course_206_le_canal_de_suez_pourquoi_il_est_strateg_p2",
      "title": "A canal everyone wants",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The canal opens in **1869**, after ten years of digging. The project is French, led by the diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps. The work is Egyptian: tens of thousands of peasants are forced to dig, and many die."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "suez_canal_first_ships_1869",
          "ratio": "1071:704",
          "caption": "1869: the first ships pass through, in a newspaper engraving of the time."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Egypt, deep in debt, soon sells its share to Britain. For the British Empire, the canal is the road to India. Its soldiers will guard it for more than seventy years."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In **July 1956**, Egypt's president, Gamal Abdel Nasser, announces that Egypt is taking the canal back. He will [[nationalize]] it and use its income to build a giant dam on the Nile."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "nasser_cairo_crowd_1956",
          "ratio": "1100:792",
          "caption": "Cairo, August 1956. Nasser is cheered by the crowd a few days after his announcement."
        },
        {
          "type": "question",
          "kind": "choice",
          "prompt": "How do Britain and France **react**?",
          "options": [
            "They accept, and negotiate a price.",
            "They attack Egypt, together with Israel.",
            "They build a second canal."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "The three countries invade in October 1956. But the United States and the Soviet Union both order them to stop. They withdraw, humiliated. The canal stays Egyptian."
        },
        {
          "type": "funFact",
          "text": "In 1967, a war between Israel and Egypt shut the canal for eight years. Fifteen cargo ships were trapped inside the whole time. Desert sand slowly covered their decks, and sailors nicknamed them the “Yellow Fleet.”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "course_206_le_canal_de_suez_pourquoi_il_est_strateg_p3",
      "title": "The world's chokepoint",
      "free": false,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Today, about **12%** of world trade passes through the canal: some 50 ships a day in a normal year. For Egypt, the tolls are worth more than 9 billion dollars a year."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "suez_canal_bridge_from_cargo_ship",
          "ratio": "1100:825",
          "caption": "The canal seen from a cargo ship. Vessels travel in convoys, one behind the other."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A passage like this has a name: a [[chokepoint]]. Everything depends on it staying open. And it does not take a ship turned sideways to close it."
        },
        {
          "type": "map",
          "map": "map_world",
          "ratio": "3:2",
          "text": "To reach the canal from Asia, ships must first pass a narrow strait at the bottom of the Red Sea: Bab el-Mandeb. From late 2023, armed groups in Yemen attack cargo ships there with missiles and drones. Many shipping companies give up on the shortcut. They send their ships the long way again, around Africa. In 2024, traffic through the canal falls by about half.",
